const emailHref = `mailto:matteo.visibility@gmail.com?subject=${encodeURIComponent(
  "Currency rejections with dLocal",
)}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  image="/images/case-studies/lofoten-coast-og.jpg"
>
  <Header />

  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />

  <main class="cs-page">
    <CaseStudyHero
      crumbLabel="Case studies"
      crumbHref="/payment-solutions/case-studies/"
      title={study.title}
      titleId="article-title"
      variant="article"
    >
      {study.region} · {study.country} · <time datetime={study.published}>{publishedLabel}</time> ·
      {study.readingMinutes} min read
    </CaseStudyHero>

    <article class="cs-article" aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <p class="cs-dek">
        Many online businesses let customers choose their currency. When those
        businesses process payments locally in Latin America with dLocal, this
        can fail completely. In the case behind this article, dLocal rejected
        every currency that the checkout offered to customers in Colombia. This
        case study explains why it happens, what dLocal's documentation says,
        and what online businesses can do about it.
      </p>

      <dl class="cs-facts-line">
        <div><dt>Region</dt><dd>{study.region} · {study.country} · South America</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Topics</dt><dd>{study.technologies.join(", ")}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>{study.role}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd><span class="cs-status-badge">{study.status}</span></dd></div>
      </dl>

      <aside class="cs-note" aria-label="About this case study">
        <strong>About this case study</strong>
        <p>
          This case study is based on a payment investigation for a company
          that I do not name. To protect that company, the example in this
          article is <em>{study.illustration}</em>. The real company is in a
          <strong>different industry</strong>. The example has the same
          characteristics that caused the problem: a checkout with a currency
          selector, and dLocal as the provider for local payments in Latin
          America. The article contains no client names, figures or internal
          details.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-note-open">
          <strong>Important:</strong> at the time of writing, the problem in the
          real case is not fully solved. This article shares the diagnosis, the
          official documentation and the options. I will update it when the
          solution is in place.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <section class="cs-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
        <h2 id="summary-title">Summary</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            dLocal processes payments in the local currency of each country.
            For Colombia, its documentation lists one currency: the Colombian
            peso (COP).
          </li>
          <li>
            If your checkout sends dLocal the currency that the customer
            selected, for example EUR or GBP, dLocal can refuse the request
            before any bank sees it. The error is 5004: "Currency not allowed
            for this country."
          </li>
          <li>
            This is not a bank decline. It is a configuration problem, so it
            can be fixed for all payments at once.
          </li>
          <li>
            The fix starts by separating three currencies: the currency you
            show, the currency you charge and the currency you receive.
          </li>
          <li>
            Two related checks: send COP amounts as whole numbers, and always
            send the customer's document number, which is required in Colombia.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <nav class="cs-toc" aria-labelledby="toc-title">
        <h2 id="toc-title">In this article</h2>
        <ol>
          {contents.map((item) => <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- 1 -->
      <section id="example" aria-labelledby="example-title">
        <h2 id="example-title">The example: a language school with a currency selector</h2>
        <p>
          In this example, an online language school sells course packages to
          students in many countries. The website has a currency selector:
          students can see prices in USD, EUR, GBP, MXN or BRL. For students in
          Latin America, the school uses dLocal to accept local cards and local
          payment methods.
        </p>
        <p>
          A student in Bogotá selects EUR and pays with a Colombian card. The
          website sends the payment to dLocal with the country CO and the
          currency EUR, and dLocal refuses it. Whatever currency the student
          selects, the result is the same. In the real case behind this
          article, this happened with every currency that the checkout offered.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          To understand why, it is useful to look at how dLocal works, and how
          it differs from a global payment provider.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2 -->
      <section id="local" aria-labelledby="local-title">
        <h2 id="local-title">Why dLocal works with local currencies</h2>
        <p>
          dLocal is a payment provider for emerging markets. Its model is local
          processing: the customer pays in the local currency with local
          payment methods, dLocal collects the money in that country, converts
          it, and pays the business in the currency that the business chooses,
          for example USD, EUR or GBP.
        </p>
        <p>
          Local processing has a clear advantage in Latin America. Many local
          cards are not enabled for international payments, and payments
          processed across borders are declined more often. Rapyd, another
          payment provider, reports that approval rates for local cards can
          rise from 20–45% with offshore processing to 80% or more with local
          processing. The exact numbers depend on the market and the business,
          but the direction is consistent across the industry.
        </p>
        <p>
          The consequence is simple: local processing means paying in the local
          currency. In Colombia, local cards, PSE bank transfers, the Nequi
          wallet and Efecty cash payments all work in Colombian pesos.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          This is the core of the problem. A checkout that lets the customer
          choose the currency is built on a different model.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3 -->
      <section id="three-currencies" aria-labelledby="three-title">
        <h2 id="three-title">Three currencies that are often mixed up</h2>
        <p>
          Every international payment involves three currencies. In many
          checkouts they are the same, so the system stores only one. With
          local processing, they must be separate.
        </p>

        <figure class="cs-currency-flow" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
          <figcaption id="flow-title">One payment, three currencies <span>(example)</span></figcaption>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span class="cs-currency-kind">1 · Display currency</span>
              <strong>EUR</strong>
              <small>What the customer sees. The customer chooses it.</small>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="cs-currency-kind">2 · Processing currency</span>
              <strong>COP</strong>
              <small>What is sent to dLocal and charged. The country decides it.</small>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="cs-currency-kind">3 · Settlement currency</span>
              <strong>USD</strong>
              <small>What the business receives. The contract decides it.</small>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The display currency (also called the presentment currency) can be
          anything you want to show. The processing currency must follow the
          rules of the country: COP for Colombia, or USD only if dLocal has made
          it available. The settlement currency is agreed in your contract with
          dLocal.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          If the system has only one currency field, it sends the display
          currency to dLocal. This is what happened in the example. The next
          section shows the rules that dLocal documents.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4 -->
      <section id="docs" aria-labelledby="docs-title">
        <h2 id="docs-title">What dLocal's documentation says</h2>
        <p>
          The following points come from dLocal's official API documentation.
          Together, they explain the rejections.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-facts" aria-labelledby="docs-facts-title">
          <figcaption id="docs-facts-title">dLocal: currency rules for Colombia</figcaption>
          <dl>
            {
              docFacts.map((fact) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
                  <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </figure>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          With these rules in mind, it is possible to diagnose the problem step
          by step.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5 -->
      <section id="diagnose" aria-labelledby="diagnose-title">
        <h2 id="diagnose-title">How to diagnose the problem: five checks</h2>
        <p>
          When payments to a market fail, I use the same five checks, in this
          order. The first check decides who must fix the problem. The other
          four find the exact cause.
        </p>
        {
          checks.map((check, index) => (
            <div class="cs-point">
              <h3>
                <span class="cs-point-number">Check {index + 1}</span>
                {check.title}
              </h3>
              {check.body.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          When these checks show a currency problem, there are five ways to
          solve it. Most businesses combine two or three of them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6 -->
      <section id="solve" aria-labelledby="solve-title">
        <h2 id="solve-title">How to solve it: five options</h2>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 1</span> Convert to the local currency at checkout</h3>
          <p>
            This is the most common solution. Keep the currency selector for
            browsing. When the customer pays with a Colombian card or a local
            payment method, convert the price to COP, show the final amount
            clearly ("You will pay COP 228,432"), and send COP to dLocal.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two details matter. First, the exchange rate: dLocal's exchange
            rate endpoint only converts from USD to a local currency, so a price
            in EUR needs another rate source, or a base price in USD. Second,
            keep a record of the original price, the rate and the COP amount for
            each order. You will need them for refunds and accounting.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">If you sell a lot in one market, you can go one step further.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 2</span> Set prices in the local currency</h3>
          <p>
            For important markets, create a price list in COP, with local price
            points such as COP 189,900 instead of an exact conversion. Customers
            see a familiar price and no conversion surprise. The trade-off is
            the exchange rate risk. dLocal Go explains it simply: when a price
            is set in USD, the customer carries the conversion cost; when it is
            set in the local currency, the business does.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Some businesses prefer to keep prices in USD. That is possible in some cases.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 3</span> Charge in USD where it is allowed</h3>
          <p>
            dLocal has a field, <code>currency_to_charge</code>, that charges
            the customer in USD. The documentation says to check availability
            by country, and the documented example is debit cards in Argentina.
            dLocal's Colombia page lists only COP, so confirm with your account
            manager before you design around this option. Also remember that
            the customer's bank converts the amount and may add its own fees.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">
            Some customers really need to pay in a foreign currency. For them,
            routing is the answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 4</span> Route by currency and card origin</h3>
          <p>
            Build a simple capability table: for each provider, country, payment
            method and currency, is the combination allowed? Before sending a
            payment, check the table. A Colombian card goes to local processing
            in COP. A customer who wants to pay in EUR with a European card goes
            to a global provider that supports EUR. The first six to eight
            digits of a card number (the BIN) tell you the country where the
            card was issued.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">Whatever option you choose, the last one is always needed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 5</span> Validate before you send, and monitor</h3>
          <p>
            Do not use the payment provider as your validator. Check the
            country, currency, amount format and document before you send the
            request. Track error 5004 as its own number, and create an alert
            when it rises. Test every combination of country, currency and
            payment method in dLocal's sandbox before you launch a market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="cs-compare" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
          <figcaption id="compare-title">The five options compared</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Option</th>
                  <th scope="col">What the customer sees</th>
                  <th scope="col">Who carries the exchange rate risk</th>
                  <th scope="col">Effort</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  options.map((option) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{option.name}</th>
                      <td>{option.sees}</td>
                      <td>{option.risk}</td>
                      <td>{option.effort}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p class="cs-bridge">
          These options all work with dLocal. To see whether the problem is
          specific to dLocal, it is useful to compare other providers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 7 -->
      <section id="providers" aria-labelledby="providers-title">
        <h2 id="providers-title">How other providers handle currency in Colombia</h2>
        <p>
          The short answer: every provider checked for this article processes
          local payments in Colombia in COP. Some also accept USD.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare" aria-labelledby="providers-table-title">
          <figcaption id="providers-table-title">Currencies for Colombia, as documented</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table class="cs-table-narrow">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Provider</th>
                  <th scope="col">Currencies for Colombia</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  providers.map((provider) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{provider.name}</th>
                      <td>{provider.currencies}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Global providers are also moving to local processing in Colombia.
          Nuvei, for example, started direct local acquiring in the country in
          2024. The lesson is clear: changing provider does not remove the rule.
          It is a property of local processing in Colombia, not a limitation of
          one provider. The solution is in how your checkout handles
          currencies.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          The same rule applies in the rest of South America, with a few
          differences.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 8 -->
      <section id="south-america" aria-labelledby="sa-title">
        <h2 id="sa-title">The same rule in other South American markets</h2>
        <p>
          dLocal's country reference shows one processing currency per country,
          and a document requirement in all of them.
        </p>
        <figure class="cs-compare" aria-labelledby="markets-title">
          <figcaption id="markets-title">South America in dLocal's country reference</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Country</th>
                  <th scope="col">Currency</th>
                  <th scope="col">Decimals</th>
                  <th scope="col">Customer document</th>
                  <th scope="col">Note</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  markets.map((market) => (
                    <tr class:list={[market.country === "Colombia" && "is-highlight"]}>
                      <th scope="row">{market.country}</th>
                      <td>{market.currency}</td>
                      <td>{market.decimals}</td>
                      <td>{market.document}</td>
                      <td>{market.note}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p>Three patterns stand out:</p>
        <ul class="cs-plain-list">
          <li>Each country has one processing currency. Ecuador is the exception that proves the rule: its official currency is the US dollar.</li>
          <li>Chile, Colombia and Paraguay use 0 decimals. A converter that always produces two decimals needs a rule per country.</li>
          <li>Every country requires a customer document, each with its own format.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          Looking ahead, a change in Colombia makes local currency even more
          important.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 9 -->
      <section id="bre-b" aria-labelledby="breb-title">
        <h2 id="breb-title">Looking ahead: Bre-B, Colombia's instant payment system</h2>
        <p>
          Colombia's central bank started the mass operation of Bre-B, its
          instant payment system, on 6 October 2025. In its first year, Bre-B
          reached 36.1 million clients and 1,796 million transactions. dLocal
          already lists Bre-B among its payment methods in Colombia.
        </p>
        <p>
          Like Pix in Brazil, Bre-B confirms payments in seconds, and it works
          in COP. For a business that already processes in COP, adding Bre-B
          later is a small step. For a business whose checkout cannot handle
          COP, it is out of reach.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">This brings the article back to its main point.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- 10 -->
      <section id="conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
        <h2 id="conclusion-title">Conclusion and next steps</h2>
        <p class="cs-takeaway">
          Your checkout can show many currencies. A payment request for a local
          market can use only the currencies that the market allows. Separate
          the currency you show from the currency you charge, and check every
          combination of country, currency and payment method before you send
          it.
        </p>
        <p>For the real case, the next steps that I recommend are:</p>
        <ol class="cs-plain-list cs-plain-list--numbered">
          <li>Confirm with dLocal which currencies are enabled for the account in each country, including USD.</li>
          <li>Add a processing currency to the order model, separate from the display currency.</li>
          <li>Choose between option 1 (convert at checkout) and option 2 (local price lists) for the largest markets.</li>
          <li>Add validation and monitoring for error 5004 before the next market launch.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section id="faq" class="cs-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
